{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>If I find there forty-five.</b> And if you ask: why at first did he diminish from fifty to forty-five, and afterward from forty-five to forty, and from forty to thirty, and from thirty to twenty, and from twenty to ten — the requests are not equal! One may answer: his requests were not to save any city at all unless ten righteous are found in it. How so? At first he asked to save Sodom, Gomorrah, Admah, and Zeboiim — in fifty righteous, who are ten per city. And afterward with forty-five, and HaShem would join to be the tenth for each city. And afterward he asked: perhaps forty will be found there, and they will save four cities; and so on — to save three cities, and likewise to save two cities, and likewise to save one of them. And less than this he did not ask, for he thought: Lot and his wife and his four daughters and his four sons-in-law are worthy to save the city on their merit. Another interpretation: when Abraham saw that HaShem agreed to the first two requests, he thought in his heart: I should not have asked for such a small amount in the manner of the tithe. For even a fifth I could have asked, since the two requests I asked amount to a fifth, the total being fifty. And therefore he now acted as though he had asked for the fifth all at once at first, and this is what he said now: perhaps forty will be found there. And afterward he asked from thirty, and after that from twenty, and after that from ten. And this is the order: at first he asked a little, and afterward more, and after that more and more. At first the two requests amounted to a fifth, and afterward he asked a fourth, and after that a third, and after that a half; and less than this he did not ask, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:50.788936034Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}